[38;2;124;124;124mLet me construct this more carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**The Second Fraenkel Model (Basic Fraenkel Model):**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m- Start with ZFA (ZF with atoms), where we have a countable set A of atoms.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- The group G is the group of all permutations of A.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- The normal filter is generated by fixators of finite subsets of A.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- A set x is in the symmetric model if there exists a finite subset E ⊆ A such [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthat every permutation fixing E pointwise also fixes x.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIn this model, the set A of atoms is in the model (it's fixed by every [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpermutation). But A cannot be linearly ordered in the model.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Why A cannot be linearly ordered in the model:**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSuppose, for contradiction, that there is a linear order ≤ on A in the model. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mThen there exists a finite subset E ⊆ A such that every permutation π fixing E [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpointwise fixes ≤ (i.e., π(≤) = ≤).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince E is finite and A is infinite (countable), there exist a, b ∈ A \ E with a[0m[38;2;124;124;124m≠ b.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mConsider the transposition π = (a b), which swaps a and b and fixes everything [0m
[38;2;124;124;124melse. Since a, b ∉ E, π fixes E pointwise, so π(≤) = ≤.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, since ≤ is a linear order on A, either a ≤ b or b ≤ a (and not both, since [0m[38;2;124;124;124ma ≠ b). WLOG say a < b (i.e., a ≤ b and a ≠ b).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince π(≤) = ≤, we have: a ≤ b ⟺ π(a) ≤ π(b) ⟺ b ≤ a.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut a < b means a ≤ b and not b ≤ a. And b ≤ a would mean b ≤ a. So a ≤ b ⟺ b ≤ [0m[38;2;124;124;124ma, which contradicts the antisymmetry (since a ≠ b, we can't have both a ≤ b and[0m[38;2;124;124;124mb ≤ a).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a contradiction. Therefore A cannot be linearly ordered in the model.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Transfer to ZF:**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBy the Jech-Sochor embedding theorem (or more precisely, the Pincus transfer [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtheorem), since the statement "there exists a set that cannot be linearly [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mordered" is a boundable statement (it's a Σ₁ statement about a set in some V_α),[0m[38;2;124;124;124mwe can transfer this to a model of ZF (without atoms).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me be more careful. The Jech-Sochor theorem says that for any [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mboundable statement, if it holds in a permutation model of ZFA, then it holds in[0m[38;2;124;124;124msome model of ZF.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe statement "there exists a set that cannot be linearly ordered" - is this [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mboundable? [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA set x cannot be linearly ordered means: for every relation r on x, r is not a [0m[38;2;124;124;124mlinear order of x. This is: ∀r ∈ P(x × x), r is not a linear order. This is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstatement about P(x × x), which lives in V_{α+1} if x ∈ V_α. So the statement "x[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcannot be linearly ordered" is a statement about V_{α+1} for some α, and "∃x [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msuch that x cannot be linearly ordered" is boundable (it's equivalent to a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstatement about some V_β).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, more precisely: "x cannot be linearly ordered" means ∀r (if r ⊆ x × x [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthen r is not a linear order on x). The quantifier ranges over P(x × x). If x ∈ [0m[38;2;124;124;124mV_α, then P(x × x) ∈ V_{α+2}. So the statement "x cannot be linearly ordered" is[0m[38;2;124;124;124ma Δ₀ statement about V_{α+2}, hence boundable. And "∃x (x cannot be linearly [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mordered)" is then a boundable statement (it's equivalent to a statement about [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mV_β for some β).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBy the Jech-Sochor embedding theorem, there exists a model of ZF in which there [0m[38;2;124;124;124mis a set that cannot be linearly ordered.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore, the Ordering Principle is not provable in ZF.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Conclusion:** No, it is not provable in ZF that every non-empty set can be [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtotally ordered. There exist models of ZF in which some set cannot be totally [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mordered.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me also note: the question says "non-empty set." The empty set is trivially [0m[38;2;124;124;124mtotally ordered (vacuously), so the restriction to non-empty sets doesn't change[0m[38;2;124;124;124manything. The set A of atoms in the Fraenkel model is non-empty (it's countably [0m[38;2;124;124;124minfinite), so the counterexample works for non-empty sets.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write up the proof cleanly.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, I should double-check: is the answer "No" (i.e., it's NOT the case [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthat every non-empty set can be totally ordered in ZF)? Yes. The Ordering [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mPrinciple is independent of ZF - it's not provable in ZF. So the answer to "Can [0m[38;2;124;124;124mevery non-empty set satisfying the axioms of ZF be totally ordered?" is No (in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe sense that ZF cannot prove this).[0m

